    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm working on a 12 week hypertrophy block for a client but they've hit a serious plateau on their back squat. they haven't added any weight in 3 weeks and their RPE is hitting 10 way too early. can you explain how deload weeks and changing up the volume might help them break through this? thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, let's tackle this plateau with a bit of a twist. You see, when a client hits a plateau, it's like a stubborn, old, grumpy goblin blocking the path to their progress. Now, deload weeks are like giving that goblin a break, a little rest, and some food. By reducing the weight and volume, you're giving your muscles and nervous system a chance to recover, which can help them grow stronger and more resilient. It's like telling the goblin, \"Hey, take a break, eat some berries, and come back stronger next time.\"\n\nChanging up the volume, on the other hand, is like changing the goblin's diet. If they've been eating the same old gruel, they won't grow. By varying the number of sets, reps, or even the exercises, you're giving their muscles a new challenge, which can help them break through that plateau. It's like saying, \"Hey goblin, let's try some new foods, or maybe even some vegetables. Surprise!\" This can help your muscles adapt and grow in new ways, helping you break through that stubborn plateau.\n\nRemember, every muscle, like every goblin, has its own unique needs and preferences. So, it's important to listen to your",
